UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 36, Lesson 2: Planning a research report', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Planning a research report means deciding headings/sections that answer the research question in a sensible order.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nPlan four section headings for: Why do shadows change length?\n\nFEEDBACK\nPossible: Introduction; Sun position; Shadow length; Conclusion — or similar logical order.\n\nCHECK\nRandom headings that ignore the question waste drafting time.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a report plan for your chosen question: title, four to five headings, two facts you hope to find under each and a sources-to-seek list.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Planning a research report means deciding headings/sections that answer the research question in a sensible order.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Writing","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-CWT-02"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a report plan for your chosen question: title, four to five headings, two facts you hope to find under each and a sources-to-seek list. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Possible: Introduction; Sun position; Shadow length; Conclusion — or similar logical order. Reasoning: I used plan by attending to sections and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Random headings that ignore the question waste drafting time. Correction: Possible: Introduction; Sun position; Shadow length; Conclusion — or similar logical order. Why: the correction restores accurate use of plan.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Random headings that ignore the question waste drafting time. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating plan on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with plan, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Possible: Introduction; Sun position; Shadow length; Conclusion — or similar logical order. This matches the lesson skill for plan.","options":["Possible: Introduction; Sun position; Shadow length; Conclusion — or similar logical order.","Random headings that ignore the question waste drafting time.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Plan four section headings for: Why do shadows change length?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Random headings that ignore the question waste drafting time. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of sections and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Random headings that ignore the question waste drafting time.","Planning a research report means deciding headings/sections that answer the research question in a sensible order.","Possible: Introduction; Sun position; Shadow length; Conclusion — or similar logical order."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named sections and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the sections and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising plan. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries plan.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show plan and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Random headings that ignore the question waste drafting time."],"question":"Read: \"Under the pier, Sam found a locked tin and a dripping map.\" Which choice best uses plan from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Random headings that ignore the question waste drafting time.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Possible: Introduction; Sun position; Shadow length; Conclusion — or similar logical order."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise sections and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Random headings that ignore the question waste drafting time.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about plan.","Retype the worked model as the final answer."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of plan to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating plan on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Possible: Introduction; Sun position; Shadow length; Conclusion — or similar logical order. Reasoning: I used plan by attending to sections and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with plan, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Random headings that ignore the question waste drafting time. Correction: Possible: Introduction; Sun position; Shadow length; Conclusion — or similar logical order. Why: the correction restores accurate use of plan.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w36-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise plan.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use plan with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify sections and explain why it matters.","I can practise heading independently on a new prompt.","I can notice report needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Planning a research report means deciding headings/sections that answer the research question in a sensible order. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Planning a research report means deciding headings/sections that answer the research question in a sensible order.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nQuestion: How do honeybees find flowers? Sections: Introduction; Bee senses; The waggle dance; Conclusion.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPlan four section headings for: Why do shadows change length?\n\nFEEDBACK\nPossible: Introduction; Sun position; Shadow length; Conclusion — or similar logical order.","explain":"Planning a research report means deciding headings/sections that answer the research question in a sensible order.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Random headings that ignore the question waste drafting time. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Report plan headed from the research question.","caption":"Headings should answer the question.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Question</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">How do honeybees find flowers?</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Sections</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Intro; senses; dance; conclusion</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Question: How do honeybees find flowers? Sections: Introduction; Bee senses; The waggle dance; Conclusion. Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Question: How do honeybees find flowers? Sections: Introduction; Bee senses; The waggle dance; Conclusion. Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nQuestion: How do honeybees find flowers? Sections: Introduction; Bee senses; The waggle dance; Conclusion. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Logical section order contrasted with random labels.","caption":"Plan before drafting.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Shadow plan</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Intro; sun position; length; conclusion</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Random</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">headings that ignore the question</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRandom headings that ignore the question waste drafting time. Here is a more productive model: Question: How do honeybees find flowers? Sections: Introduction; Bee senses; The waggle dance; Conclusion.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nPossible: Introduction; Sun position; Shadow length; Conclusion — or similar logical order.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Random headings that ignore the question waste drafting time. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPlanning a research report means deciding headings/sections that answer the research question in a sensible order. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 36, Lesson 2: Planning a research report","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Research project and portfolio","week":36,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nQuestion: How do honeybees find flowers? Sections: Introduction; Bee senses; The waggle dance; Conclusion.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Report plan headed from the research question.","caption":"Headings should answer the question.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Question</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">How do honeybees find flowers?</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Sections</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Intro; senses; dance; conclusion</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Logical section order contrasted with random labels.","caption":"Plan before drafting.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Shadow plan</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Intro; sun position; length; conclusion</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Random</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">headings that ignore the question</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-eng-y3-w36-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
